A||||12||Fierokid87||Tranny decision||||1||
Z||000000||Fierokid87||12-25-2001||06:21 PM||||Ok all Im lookign for a tranny here.  Ill probably stick with the 3 speed auto.  Its easier on my wallet as well as easier on time and effort.  I knwo Im lazy that way.  But Im installing a the 2.8 V-6 in my could and I would liek to knwo what tranny i shoudl use.  the 3.33 FD or the 3.18 FD.  Which one is better?  And what one would everyone else use for a V-6 <P>Scott<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="red"> Fierokid's  Web site:</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="white">http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero</FONT><P><FONT COLOR="blue">AIM: Fierokid87<BR>ICQ: Fierokid</FONT><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierokid.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||tomcat06085||12-25-2001||06:48 PM||||I'm setting up a 1988 GT for autocross and would like to know how much abuse the automatic three speed will take. Has anyone used either of the above mentioned ratios in competition events. Also, I'm curious about what kind of speeds to expect from 1st gear, 2nd gear and 3rd if it's locked in gear and the engine is revved to about 5500 rpm's||||reg||5
Z||000002||mrfiero||12-25-2001||07:21 PM||||Well, the 3.18 will give you more top end, but the 3.33 will give you better acceleration.  How big the difference really is.....I don't know.<P>There was also a 2.87 ratio (in the 4-cyls.) and I think there was a 3.06 too (in the '85 V-6?).<P>At any rate, I would go with the 3.33 myself.  My low mileage '88 GT has this ratio and it helps in the accelration department (and with a slushbox I need all the help I can get!).<P>While I haven't had my GT to top speed kin a LONG time, I believe 5500 RPMs are not attainable with an auto. in stock form......I'm thinking 5000 is where it maxes out (at least with the 3.33 gears).<P><BR>I forgot to mention.....the auto. trannies, while not a good performance choice per se, are darn near bullet proof.  Unless you have a really high mileage unit you should have no problems.  I have owned/driven auto. Fieros with 10,000 miles to almost 200,000 miles and none of them failed me.<P>I even abused the heck out of an auto. '88 GT by towing a dozen cars cross country and it still shifted fine (just changed the fluid more frequently).<P>  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 12-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierokid87||12-25-2001||10:49 PM||||So what is better then?  I mean what would I want to get if I want accelleration and top end.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000004||Two88GTs||12-25-2001||11:07 PM||||Hehehe, I've had a couple of chances to wind out both of my 3.33 equipped automatic 88GTs this summer on the highway.  I think the speedo went as high as 117 before the tach neared the 5500 rpm mark(I couldn't bring myself to rev any faster and I had to slow down to make my exit).||||reg||1
Z||000005||voyagerspe||12-26-2001||01:01 AM||||how do you identify the different ratios?  Is it marked somewhere on the case?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierokid87||12-26-2001||03:43 PM||||How good will the tack off be with the 3.18, and how will top end be?  What car did the 3.18 come in?  Was it in 85 4 cyld models?<P>Scott<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="red"> Fierokid's  Web site:</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="white">http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero</FONT><P><FONT COLOR="blue">AIM: Fierokid87<BR>ICQ: Fierokid</FONT><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierokid.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierokid87||12-26-2001||10:49 PM||||Bumpity bump bump||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||12-26-2001||11:03 PM||||I know the 3.33's were only available on '87 & '88 V-6's.  I'm pretty sure all 4-cyls. got the 2.87's.  I believe the '85-'86 V-6's got the 3.18's, but I could have sworn there was a 3.06 ratio in theer somewhere.<P>I'll try to pinpoint this for you tomorrow.||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroRacer||12-27-2001||01:32 AM||||84-86 3.18, 3.06<BR>87-88 2.84, 3.33<P>Transaxle Codes<BR>PF, 6PF 3.18<BR>CD, 6CD 3.06<BR>7CPC, 8CPC 3.33<BR>7PSC, 8PSC 2.84<P>Model code should be somewhere near the Bellhousing cover.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fierokid87||12-27-2001||07:17 PM||||Well still the question remains.  What ratio woudl everyone go with?  3.18?  Or 3.33?  I know the 3.18 would give good top end.  But what kind of take off will it give with a modded 2.8?<P>Scott<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="red"> Fierokid's  Web site:</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="white">http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero</FONT><P><FONT COLOR="blue">AIM: Fierokid87<BR>ICQ: Fierokid</FONT><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierokid.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||12-27-2001||07:42 PM||||Funny you should ask... I'm about to make this even harder for you I'm afraid......<P>There be a new page in the tranny section of my cave.<P>Part of your question will be decided by your tire choice... <P>If you are going to run tires with a significantly larger rolling diameter than stock,,,, you'll likely want the 3.33 gears. Depending on exactly how much larger they are vs. original, you may still loose performance at launch... the plus is you'll cruise at a lower RPM.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fierokid87||12-27-2001||10:29 PM||||thanks Orge.  your a Big help to the Fiero community.<P>Scott||||reg||1
